blanks—straight to decode.Parse, so I need to add blank-line skipping there too.\n\nTracing through batch's Run, splitting on newlines produces empty strings interspersed with valid entries, and an empty input string yields a single blank line, both of which require the skip-blank fix in service.Run to total correctly.\n\nNow I'm settling on the fix for report.go: formatting the total as \"total=\" followed by the count and a newline.\n\nFor the ledger package, I'm defining a Ledger struct with a running total and a seen-IDs map for deduplication, plus an Apply method that validates the event kind, skips already-seen IDs, and adds or subtracts the amount based on whether it's a credit or debit...\n\nAdding a Total accessor...\n\nNow for decode, I'm parsing a JSON line into an Event struct and validating that the ID isn't empty, the kind is either \"credit\" or \"debit\", and the amount is strictly positive, returning errors otherwise.\n\nI notice some overlap since the service tests also check invalid kind and negative amounts, but decode's validation already catches those cases, so that's fine. In service.Run, I'm iterating over lines, skipping blank ones, and calling decode.Parse on each, returning early on error.\n\nThen in Apply I'm applying each entry to the ledger and returning errors as needed, finishing with report.Format on the total. For main.go, I'm reading stdin, calling batch.Run, writing errors to stderr with a nonzero exit code, otherwise printing the result. Now I want to check the ledger_test.go expectations more carefully to make sure everything lines up.\n\nTracing through the test cases confirms my ledger logic: credits add, debits subtract, duplicate IDs are ignored, and invalid kinds return an error without mutating the total. The service_test.go cases check that malformed lines (broken syntax, empty ID, invalid kind, negative amount) all produce decode errors, while valid deduplicated entries produce the correct \"total=5\" output.\n\nBatch an